 
//user macros slice 3 landed: `macro name(p: Expr, n: i32){...}` + `name!(...)`
//-- an `Expr` parameter is a syntax slot (each reference is replaced by a copy
//of the argument, the body is copied per call site), any other declared type is
//a value parameter (bound once, typed, at the top of the expansion). Statement
//+ expression position; the names the body declares are alpha-renamed per call
//site; lookup follows the module rules (`m::name!(...)`, `use m;` /
//`use m::{name}`, top-level macros of imported units).
//free body names (never declared, not a parameter) resolve at the call site; a
//caller *local* of that spelling is now an error instead of a capture
//(2026-10-19), still open is the other half of definition-site resolution: a
//module-level name that the macro's unit declares but the caller also has
//(global/function/same-named import) still resolves at the call site.
//code-generation macros -- doc/
//codegen_macros.txt is the design note: declarative list parameters +
//`$( ... )*` repetition (no CTFE, no token-tree matcher). Phase A landed
//2026-10-19 in both positions (`xs: Expr...`, `$xs`, `$( stmts )*`,
//`$( exprs ),*`, tests/normal/47-macro-repeat.x and 48-macro-repeat-expr.x);
//still open: item position (phase B), fragment kinds (C), non-Expr list
//elements, struct-literal entries. doc/token_macros.txt keeps the state of
//the unused TokenStream/#derive_macro stub, and the live derive generators are
//in derive.x.

//module members of another unit: after `import dep`, `dep`-side `m::T` /
//`m::f()` reports "a module's members are only visible in the unit that
//declares them" (doc/modules.txt). A member's own type is leaf-only (`S`, not
//`m::S`) and its declaration lives in the other unit, so making it work needs
//the member type qualified plus emitter support for the foreign decl; it used
//to panic in get_decl. Scoped cross-unit *macro* calls already work.

//closures: `|a: i32|: i32{ .. }` and `||` lambdas work, including capture
//(phase A, 2026-10-20): a body may read an enclosing local or parameter *by
//value*; the value is a heap closure object and its drop frees it
//(tests/normal/49-closure-capture.x, design note doc/closures.txt section 8).
//The `(a: i32): i32 => { .. }` form is abandoned (user, 2026-10-20).
//Still open: capturing an owning value ("capturing an owning value is not
//supported yet"), capture through a nested lambda ("capturing through a lambda
//is not supported yet"), `self` capture and by-reference capture.
//(fixed 2026-10-21) a lambda that *returns* a struct used to crash: the method
//uses the sret ABI while the lambda-call prototype returned the struct by
//value. tests/normal/61-lambda-struct-return.x.
//typed float literals are fixed (2026-10-20): `let k: f64 = 2.5;` and a f64
//call argument/field work; a float literal into an integer type is a clean
//"2.5 is a float literal, not i32" (tests/normal/50-float-literal-cast.x).


//panic macro: a user macro with a value parameter now works, but `panic` is a
//reserved builtin macro name; the shape is e.g. `macro my_panic(e: Expr, s: str)`


func to_list<E, T>(it: T): List<E>
call like to_list<i32>(..) should work
//2026-10-20 verified: still open, and it is about *partial* type arguments --
//`to_list<i32>(&l)` is "to_list(IT) type arg size mismatched", while
//`to_list<i32, i32>(&l)` resolves and runs. Needs inference for the omitted
//tail of the type-argument list. (Nested `match` was re-checked the same day
//and works, so that todo line is gone.)

//compile-time profile (2026-10-21, after the -O0 pin came off; numbers in
//handoff.md): 41.5% LLVM, 38.3% libc (malloc/free/memcpy), 11.9% x std helpers
//on a 25-file -O2 module compile. The front end's cost is allocation churn and
//str hashing/comparison, not parser/resolver logic. Concrete, unstarted:
//Instruction-level shaving in std does not reach the wall clock (measured, see
//handoff.md): dropping the per-element loop in `List::drop_elems` (1.6% of Ir)
//and reserving 32 bytes in `Fmt::new` (-33k of 1.2M allocations) both left the
//time unchanged. What is left, in order:
//1. fewer clones/allocations structurally: `String::clone` is 698k allocations
//   per 25-file compile; `Type::print` (89k) returns an owned String on the
//   mangling path where appending into the caller's buffer would do;
//   `clone_simple`/`Desc::clone` clone per lookup.
//2. front-end arena for short-lived strings (malloc+free+memcpy = 38% of the
//   model); a design change, needs a decision.
//3. small: keyword lookup without `HashMap<str,TokenType>` (487k probes per
//   compile, half of `str::hash`'s instructions).
//`#[inline]` in std (2026-10-21): now on `List::get`/`get_internal` and
//`Option::get`/`is_some`/`is_none`, worth -5% on a 2M-append String scan at
//default -O with no compile cost (handoff.md, batch 16). Non-generic helpers
//(`str::len`, `String::len`) cannot inline across units because std is a
//separate archive, so annotating them buys nothing for user code. Not tried:
//`List::add`, `HashMap::get` -- larger bodies, measure first.

//"-O0.5" codegen pipeline: landed 2026-10-21 as `globaldce,mem2reg,dce`
//(+`always-inline` when `#[inline]` is used). A 200M-iteration local loop went
//431ms -> 88ms, the compile-time cost was nil (2222 vs 2218ms on a 25-file
//module set), and `bin/test.sh pipeline` pins it on the emitted IR. See
//handoff.md for the numbers. Still open: `instcombine` cannot be dropped into
//a textual pipeline (it aborts: "Instruction Combining did not reach a
//fixpoint after 1 iterations"), so a later step could add it through a proper
//pass-builder pipeline and check whether the extra folding pays.
